  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Rewrite the equations of the circles in standard form.\n2. Find the centers and radii of the circles.\n3. Understand that the tangent line will have a specific relationship with the line connecting the centers.\n4. Use the properties of perpendicular lines and tangents to find the direction vector of the tangent line.\n5. Write the equation of the line using the appropriate slope and point.\n\n### Step 1: Rewrite the equations in standard form\nThe general form of the circle equation is \\(x^2 + y^2 + Dx + Ey + F = 0\\), and we rewrite the given equations as follows:\n\nFor \\(C_1: x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 4y + 1 = 0\\):\nThe center \\((h_1, k_1)\\) is \\((1, 2)\\) and the radius \\(r_1\\) is \\(\\sqrt{1^2 + 2^2 - 1} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\\).\n\nFor \\(C_2: x^2 + y^2 + 2x - 6y + 2 = 0\\):\nThe center \\((h_2, k_2)\\) is \\((-1, 3)\\) and the radius \\(r_2\\) is \\(\\sqrt{(-1)^2 + 3^2 - 2} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the centers and radii\nThe centers of the circles are \\(C_1 = (1, 2)\\) and \\(C_2 = (-1, 3)\\), and their radii are \\(r_1 = 2\\) and \\(r_2 = 2\\sqrt{2}\\) respectively.\n\n### Step 3: Understand the perpendicular line\nThe line segment \\(P_1P_2\\) is perpendicular to the line passing through the centers of \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\). The direction vector of the line segment \\(P_1P_2\\) can be found using the relative differences between the coordinates of the points of tangency. \n\nThe normal vector to the tangent circles at the centers \\(C_1(C_1, C_2)\\) and \\(C_2(C_2, C_1)\\) will give us the direction vector of the tangents. Since the tangent line \\(P_1P_2\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(C_1C_2\\), we can find the direction vector of \\(P_1P_2\\) by subtracting the coordinates of the centers.\n\n### Step 4: Find the direction vector\nThe direction vector of the line segment \\(P_1P_2\\) is given by the difference in the coordinates of the points of tangency:\n\\[\n\\vec{d} = \\vec{OP_2} - \\vec{OP_1} = (-1 - 1, 3 - 2) = (-2, 1).\n\\]\nHowever, since the line segment is perpendicular to the line \\(C_1C_2\\), we need to normalize this vector. The direction vector of the tangent line is \\((-2, 1)\\) after normalization:\n\\[\n\\vec{t} = \\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right).\n\\]\nThe magnitude is \\(\\sqrt{5}\\).\n\n### Step 5: Write the equation of the line\nThe tangent line passing through the point of tangency \\(P_1\\left(1, 2\\right)\\) and having the direction vector \\((-2, 1)\\) can be written using the point-slope form of the line equation:\n\\[\ny - 2 = -2(x - 1).\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\ny - 2 = -2x + 2,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -2x + 4.\n\\]\nRewriting in standard form:\n\\[\n2x + y - 4 = 0.\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe equation of the line is \\(\\boxed{2x + y - 4 = 0}\\).",
